0002-highscore-advisory-lock-best-effort.patch

highscore.c do_open() takes a POSIX advisory record lock (fcntl F_SETLKW with
a struct flock) on the score file, and treats a lock failure as fatal():
   if (res == -1) fatal ("Cannot lock score file ...");

MayteraOS's libc carries the fcntl lock constants and struct flock for source
compatibility only: the kernel has no multi-process advisory record locking and
returns -1/ENOSYS (see libc fcntl.h, added for the angband port, whose callers
already ignore the return). moon-buggy is the odd one out in that it checks the
return and aborts, so the game would fatal the first time it writes a score.

MayteraOS runs a single writer, so the lock is unnecessary. This patch makes
the lock best-effort: attempt it (harmless), but do not abort on failure,
matching the advisory-locking-is-best-effort contract every other caller in the
tree relies on. Score reading/writing is otherwise unchanged.

--- a/highscore.c	2026-09-08 14:53:44.656381642 +0000
+++ b/highscore.c	2026-09-08 14:53:44.657324135 +0000
@@ -381,9 +381,11 @@
     do {
       res = fcntl (fd, F_SETLKW, &l);
     } while (res == -1 && errno == EINTR);
-    if (res == -1) {
-      fatal ("Cannot lock score file \"%s\": %s", name, strerror (errno));
-    }
+    /* MayteraOS port: advisory record locking is source-compatible only (the
+     * kernel returns -1/ENOSYS, see libc fcntl.h) and MayteraOS runs a single
+     * writer, so a failed lock is not fatal here, matching the best-effort
+     * contract every other caller in the tree already relies on.  */
+    (void) res;
   }
   
   return  fd;
